2026-10-19 21:14 Implementation note (mcp-hub-worker).

What changed
- lib/MCP/Hub/Upstream/Stdio.pm: _on_close remembers the pid it handled
  ({exited_pid}); the catch in start_p records the exit only when that pid is
  not the one of the current attempt -- so a child that dies during the
  handshake is counted once, by _on_close, and a handshake timeout (the child
  is still alive) is counted once, by the catch.
- {stopping} is now set only while a pid exists. Before, the catch set it for
  a child that was already gone; the flag stayed set and made the next
  unexpected exit look deliberate -- no warning, no exit recorded.
- _spawn clears {exited_pid}, so a new attempt is never compared against the
  pid of an older one.

Tests
- t/stdio.t: 'a child that dies at startup is counted once'. A command that
  exits immediately: stopped after the first and the second attempt, failed
  after the third. The log is captured as well: every attempt logs the
  'child exited' warning, which the stale stopping flag used to swallow.

Verified
- prove -lr t/ green, twice (16 files, 135 tests).
- Red against the old lib: t/stdio.t subtest 8 fails there -- 'stopped after
  attempt 2' and 'attempt 2 logs the exit' are the failures; the old code
  counted the exit twice and went failed after two attempts, exactly as the
  card's repro says.

Not changed
- The three-exits-in-five-seconds rule itself, the crash-loop handling of a
  child that dies during a call, and the deliberate stop are untouched: a
  deliberate stop during the handshake is now not counted as an exit either,
  which is what the flag means.

